### 1. `src/client/world/remote-ships.ts`

```ts
import * as THREE from 'three';
import type { Livery } from '@shared/protocol/schemas';
import { applyLivery, buildShipMesh, disposeShipMesh, type ShipMesh } from '@client/render/ship-mesh';

/**
 * TASK-74: ... ship rendering for the remote layer (per-kind module pattern).
 */

/** Hostile trim for AI ships — a single constant to distinguish from player ships. */
export const AI_SHIP_TRIM_COLOR = '#e5484d';
/** Label anchor height above the ship's origin (m — above the 0.9 m dorsal fin). */
export const SHIP_LABEL_HEIGHT_M = 2;

export interface ShipRender {
  mesh: ShipMesh;
  group: THREE.Group;
  /** kind 'ai-ship' → the trim zone is always forced to AI_SHIP_TRIM_COLOR. */
  ai: boolean;
  /** The last applied livery key (dedup guard). */
  liveryKey: string;
}

export function liveryKey(livery: Livery | undefined): string {
  return livery ? Object.entries(livery).sort((a,b)=>a[0].compareTo(b[0])).map(([k,v])=>`${k}=${v}`).join(';') : '';
}

export function createShipRender(classId: string, ai: boolean): ShipRender {
  const mesh = buildShipMesh(classId);
  for (const mat of Object.values(mesh.zones)) mat.transparent = true;
  const render: ShipRender = { mesh, group: mesh.group, ai, liveryKey: liveryKey(undefined) };
  applyShipLivery(render, undefined);
  return render;
}
```

Wait: createShipRender with a livery — the layer holds info.livery and will apply it on the first frame. Simpler: create without livery (class defaults + AI trim), and let the per-frame dedup apply the wire livery on the first frame.

```ts
/** Re-tint in place. No-op if the livery hasn't changed. AI ships always end with hostile trim. */
export function applyShipLivery(render: ShipRender, livery: Livery | undefined): void {
  const key = liveryKey(livery);
  if (key === render.liveryKey) return;
  render.liveryKey = key;
  applyLivery(render.mesh, livery);
  if (render.ai) render.mesh.zones.trim.color.set(AI_SHIP_TRIM_COLOR);
}
```

Wait, dedup semantics: "tint via applyLivery only when the wire livery changes (dedup similar to character tinting)". The key is just the livery. But for an AI ship with no livery, the initial build applies the class defaults, then the trim is forced. First frame: livery is undefined, key is '' — same as initial liveryKey '' → no-op, fine, the build already handled the default + trim. But note: on creation, set liveryKey = liveryKey(info.livery) and apply once? Let's make it so we apply the current livery once at creation: createShipRender(classId, ai, livery) → build, set transparent, applyShipLivery(render, livery). Then on subsequent frames dedup works. And for classId change: the layer detects a classId mismatch → dispose + rebuild with current livery.

Hmm, but applyShipLivery's dedup uses liveryKey — for the initial creation with a livery: liveryKey starts as '' (or a sentinel), apply → sets key. Let's initialize with liveryKey = null as `string | null`.

```ts
export function setShipOpacity(render: ShipRender, opacity: number): void {
  for (const mat of Object.values(render.mesh.zones)) mat.opacity = opacity;
}
export function disposeShipRender(render: ShipRender): void {
  disposeShipMesh(render.mesh);
}
```

Note: disposeShipMesh disposes geometries + materials and clears the group. Good.

One concern: zone materials are shared within one ship (one material per zone) — disposeShipMesh disposes each zone material once. Good, no double-dispose since it only traverses geometries.

